00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:32.670
So the cool thing of
the week is introducing

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:36.044
on Google Container Engine--
we have now got node pools.

00:01:36.044 --> 00:01:36.960
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:37.770
MARK MANDEL: Which
is really cool.

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:39.620
So what that really
means is, you can now

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:41.230
do heterogeneous clusters.

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:43.856
So if you want to have
different types of machines

00:01:43.856 --> 00:01:46.230
within your clusters, so
they're not all the same-- maybe

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:48.700
you have some with more
memory, some with more CPU--

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:51.540
you're able to do that and
then have that all exist

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:53.170
within the same
cluster and basically

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:54.400
be able to turn around
and say, like, I

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:56.120
want these containers
to be running

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:58.220
on these sort of
high-mem ones, or I

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:00.944
want these ones to be running
on, say, these high-CPU ones.

00:02:00.944 --> 00:02:03.360
And so you can have a little
bit more flexibility in terms

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:04.776
of what sort of
resources you have

00:02:04.776 --> 00:02:06.590
available to you in
your GKE cluster.

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:08.860
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, and
I think that since once you

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:13.420
have more than two
node pools, well,

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:15.940
more than one node
pool, actually, you're

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:20.420
able to tag those
nodes accordingly.

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:22.360
And then basically
say, I want this

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.640
to be running on
some of those nodes

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:27.000
that have the tag high-mem
or whatever you want to do.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:27.750
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:27.810
Yeah.

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:29.750
No, they actually get
defaultly tagged just

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:32.020
really nice with the
new nodes and like what

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:33.300
they are when you create them.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:33.760
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Perfect.

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:36.135
MARK MANDEL: I actually really
like this cute trick here.

00:02:36.135 --> 00:02:39.060
And it's in the blog
post we're talking about.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:41.730
If you want to do an upgrade
from one version of Kubernetes

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:44.700
to another, the new node
pools have the new version.

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:46.982
So you can actually try
out your stuff on there

00:02:46.982 --> 00:02:48.940
before you upgrade the
rest of your other nodes

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:50.610
in your cluster.

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:52.950
So you can do some really
clever things that way, too.

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:54.614
Yeah, it's actually pretty cool.

00:02:54.614 --> 00:02:55.905
It's a really nice new feature.

00:02:55.905 --> 00:02:57.363
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is very cool.

00:02:57.363 --> 00:03:00.550
I actually think it's a very
good match for Google Cloud

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:01.050
Spin.

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:03.480
Again, I will keep
on talking about it.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:06.900
Because it's actually when
you need to extract something

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:10.370
from frame, you need a specific
amount of memory and so on.

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:12.810
But then when you're
stitching stuff together,

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:14.919
you actually need more
CPU rather than anything.

00:03:14.919 --> 00:03:15.710
MARK MANDEL: Right.

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:18.329
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So you
could have different nodes.

00:03:18.329 --> 00:03:19.120
That's pretty cool.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:19.330
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:21.070
You can be really efficient
with your workload.

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:23.153
So you're only paying for
really the certain stuff

00:03:23.153 --> 00:03:23.970
that you need.

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:25.095
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

