it one more time

00:09:07.060 --> 00:09:08.940
because there's a number of
people who when they hear free,

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:09.610
don't believe it.

00:09:09.610 --> 00:09:10.230
So is it free?

00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:13.600
DOUG STEVENSON: OK, it's
free as in F-R-E-E, like--

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:14.750
MARK MANDEL: As in beer.

00:09:14.750 --> 00:09:15.833
DAN STEVENSON: As in beer.

00:09:15.833 --> 00:09:17.460
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:19.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
unlimited for real.

00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:21.130
DOUG STEVENSON: Unlimited,
so you can send as much data

00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:21.990
into it as you want.

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:22.360
Now--

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:23.020
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is an amazing.

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:24.350
DOUG STEVENSON: --And Crash
Reporting is like that too.

00:09:24.350 --> 00:09:26.141
Crash Reporting is also
free and unlimited,

00:09:26.141 --> 00:09:28.260
but it's worth
noting as a caveat,

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:31.190
you can't just jam
as much data as you--

00:09:31.190 --> 00:09:33.540
you can't just spin up as
much data as you possibly can.

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:35.860
There is reasonable
usage patterns

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:37.630
which we can reasonably
handle, you know,

00:09:37.630 --> 00:09:39.637
you can't force
anything beyond that.

00:09:39.637 --> 00:09:41.470
ABE HASKINS: There's
abuse vectors in place,

00:09:41.470 --> 00:09:44.434
but for all practical uses,
it is free and unlimited.

00:09:44.434 --> 00:09:45.350
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:09:45.350 --> 00:09:46.310
That is very impressive.

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:47.970
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
what's sort of use cases

00:09:47.970 --> 00:09:49.860
do you think you're going to
see people using Analytics for?

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:51.520
Have you seen anything already?

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:53.540
ABE HASKINS: I mean,
you can imagine

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:59.130
that this is Google's version
of Google Analytics for Mobile,

00:09:59.130 --> 00:10:01.665
and you can-- we have
more than a decade

00:10:01.665 --> 00:10:03.790
of people doing crazy things
with Google Analytics,

00:10:03.790 --> 00:10:08.100
and we expect fully to
see all of those times 100

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:09.290
with Firebase Analytics.

00:10:09.290 --> 00:10:12.790
The amount of flexibility we
introduce with these custom

00:10:12.790 --> 00:10:14.876
events and the ability to
do all these funnelings

00:10:14.876 --> 00:10:16.250
and notifications
and things like

00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:19.730
that, it's just-- it's huge,
and it's very open-ended.

00:10:19.730 --> 00:10:22.810
I haven't seen
anything specifically

00:10:22.810 --> 00:10:25.660
that sticks out because
it's still very early,

00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:28.540
but I'm sure we're going to see
tons and tons of crazy things.

00:10:28.540 --> 00:10:30.290
Plus, it's free, and
that always brings up

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:32.340
people who build
crazy stuff on things.

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:33.430
DOUG STEVENSON: Yeah, one of
the things that people really

00:10:33.430 --> 00:10:35.340
want to do is they
want to identify

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:37.560
who their big spenders
are so there's a concept

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:39.350
of lifetime value of a user.

00:10:39.350 --> 00:10:42.190
Then you can, again,
use that to target them

00:10:42.190 --> 00:10:45.850
with, say, notifications,
or put them in A/B testing

00:10:45.850 --> 00:10:48.370
with Remote Config based on
their prior behavior, which

00:10:48.370 --> 00:10:49.497
is huge for a lot of apps.

00:10:49.497 --> 00:10:51.080
MARK MANDEL: That
actually segues just

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:52.130
into what I wanted
to talk about.

00:10:52.130 --> 00:10:53.880
Do you want to talk a little
about Remote Config and what

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:55.084
that is, and how that works?

00:10:55.084 --> 00:10:56.750
DOUG STEVENSON: Yeah,
so Remote Config--

00:10:56.750 --> 00:10:59.640
you can basically think of it as
like a switchboard in the sky,

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:00.140
right?

00:11:00.140 --> 00:11:02.035
So you can-- I know
it's kind of a goofy way

00:11:02.035 --> 00:11:02.910
of thinking about it.

00:11:02.910 --> 00:11:05.470
MARK MANDEL: Trying to
imagine that right now.

00:11:05.470 --> 00:11:06.570
DOUG STEVENSON: So what
you can do-- I mean,

00:11:06.570 --> 00:11:07.760
there's a few things
you can do with it.

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:09.676
I think the most popular
thing is A/B testing.

00:11:09.676 --> 00:11:12.169
So you can have two different
experiences in your app.

00:11:12.169 --> 00:11:13.960
And you want to pit
them against each other

00:11:13.960 --> 00:11:15.470
to see which one
makes more money,

00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:19.490
or which one has higher
retention, or has better,

00:11:19.490 --> 00:11:21.750
you know, play
through for a game.

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:24.490
So you can run that test,
you can define percentages.

00:11:24.490 --> 00:11:25.905
You could say
50/50 of people get

00:11:25.905 --> 00:11:27.530
one experience over
the other, and then

00:11:27.530 --> 00:11:29.914
measure the outcome
in Analytics.

00:11:29.914 --> 00:11:31.330
The other thing
that people can do

00:11:31.330 --> 00:11:34.820
is, say, roll out a
new feature instantly.

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:37.694
So a prior problem that
app developers had is they

00:11:37.694 --> 00:11:39.610
had to submit to the
store, and then the store

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:42.068
doesn't give you a guarantee
about how long that, you know,

00:11:42.068 --> 00:11:43.580
the new feature gets out.

00:11:43.580 --> 00:11:45.222
Both Android and iOS
have this problem.

00:11:45.222 --> 00:11:46.680
But with Remote
Config, what you do

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:49.290
is you code up your feature
early, publish it ahead

00:11:49.290 --> 00:11:51.492
of time, then when you're
confident that the app is

00:11:51.492 --> 00:11:52.950
actually on the
user's devices, you

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:54.510
can use Remote Config
to flip a switch

00:11:54.510 --> 00:11:56.690
and then everyone gets the new
experience of the new feature

00:11:56.690 --> 00:11:57.200
instantly.

00:11:57.200 --> 00:11:59.440
So you can time that with,
like, a television event,

00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:01.194
or a tweet, or
something like that,

00:12:01.194 --> 00:12:03.610
to make sure that everyone
gets the experience at the time

00:12:03.610 --> 00:12:04.570
that you want them to have it.

00:12:04.570 --> 00:12:06.445
ABE HASKINS: Yeah, and
it's not just flipping

00:12:06.445 --> 00:12:08.507
switches that you
manually go in and set.

00:12:08.507 --> 00:12:11.090
You can do that where you say,
I'm going to change this value,

00:12:11.090 --> 00:12:13.480
and then users get some
different experience.

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:16.534
But you can do things where you
say people located in Australia

00:12:16.534 --> 00:12:18.950
are going to get a different
home page than people located

00:12:18.950 --> 00:12:19.900
in the US.

00:12:19.900 --> 00:12:21.650
People running an older
version of Android

00:12:21.650 --> 00:12:24.130
might get a warning saying
you need to upgrade.

00:12:24.130 --> 00:12:26.190
Otherwise, we're not
going to support this.

00:12:26.190 --> 00:12:27.660
There's a bunch of these
different categories

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:29.118
and buckets that
you can filter on,

00:12:29.118 --> 00:12:32.249
so just arbitrary percentage
for A/B testing, that's there.

00:12:32.249 --> 00:12:34.040
But we have a whole
bunch of different ways

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:35.910
to send different values
to different people.

00:12:35.910 --> 00:12:37.910
DOUG STEVENSON: Yeah, the
other thing you can do

00:12:37.910 --> 00:12:40.392
is have your strings in your
app defined in Remote Config.

00:12:40.392 --> 00:12:42.350
So you can say in different
parts of the world,

00:12:42.350 --> 00:12:43.307
change the experience.

00:12:43.307 --> 00:12:45.140
Like, for Android, I'm
an Android developer,

00:12:45.140 --> 00:12:46.440
so you can put
that in resources,

00:12:46.440 --> 00:12:48.606
but you can't-- once you've
baked in those resources

00:12:48.606 --> 00:12:50.650
into the app, they're
there forever, right?

00:12:50.650 --> 00:12:52.150
With Remote Config,
now you can make

00:12:52.150 --> 00:12:53.816
those say whatever
you want, at the time

00:12:53.816 --> 00:12:56.180
that you want, in the part
of the world that you want.

00:12:56.180 --> 00:12:57.060
MARK MANDEL: I was
going say this sounds

00:12:57.060 --> 00:12:57.700
particular powerful.

00:12:57.700 --> 00:12:58.510
You're talking
about sort of being

00:12:58.510 --> 00:13:00.972
able to build your own
audiences within site analytics,

00:13:00.972 --> 00:13:03.180
like say, people have gone
past level 3, or something

00:13:03.180 --> 00:13:05.550
like that, and then being able
to combine that with Remote

00:13:05.550 --> 00:13:06.860
Config as well or being
able to say, like,

00:13:06.860 --> 00:13:08.200
these particular
audiences are going

00:13:08.200 --> 00:13:10.260
to see this particular thing,
which is different from people

00:13:10.260 --> 00:13:11.725
who, say, have just
started, or are

00:13:11.725 --> 00:13:13.680
less experienced in
the game, or whatever

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:14.730
application you've got.

00:13:14.730 --> 00:13:16.207
That sounds like
really cool stuff.

00:13:16.207 --> 00:13:17.790
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
I have a question.

00:13:17.790 --> 00:13:20.180
So if I understand correctly,
the main difference

00:13:20.180 --> 00:13:22.490
between using-- so
if you want to store

00:13:22.490 --> 00:13:25.280
some configuration that
might change over time,

00:13:25.280 --> 00:13:28.110
you have the option
between storing it

00:13:28.110 --> 00:13:30.080
in the Realtime
Database directly,

00:13:30.080 --> 00:13:32.280
or store it in Remote Config.

00:13:32.280 --> 00:13:35.940
What is the big difference
between doing one or the other?

00:13:35.940 --> 00:13:38.350
ABE HASKINS: I think there's
a handful of differences.

00:13:38.350 --> 00:13:41.540
The Realtime Database is
designed for small amounts

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:42.731
of quickly changing data.

00:13:42.731 --> 00:13:44.980
That's always been the focus
of the Realtime Database.

00:13:44.980 --> 00:13:48.515
So if you have huge strings
that have descriptions

00:13:48.515 --> 00:13:49.890
in different
languages, you could

00:13:49.890 --> 00:13:53.100
put those in the Realtime
Database, that would be fine.

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:54.980
But if you're going and
you're filtering them

00:13:54.980 --> 00:13:57.940
based on language or
something, otherwise,

00:13:57.940 --> 00:13:59.350
like once they
get to the device,

00:13:59.350 --> 00:14:01.410
you might as well do
that in a system that

00:14:01.410 --> 00:14:02.760
serves that up automatically.

00:14:02.760 --> 00:14:04.679
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:14:04.679 --> 00:14:06.720
ABE HASKINS: There's few
things that you couldn't

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:09.220
do if you wanted to jam
into the Realtime Database

00:14:09.220 --> 00:14:12.412
and then do this
filtering on your own,

00:14:12.412 --> 00:14:14.120
but it makes more
sense and the interface

00:14:14.120 --> 00:14:15.494
is better for this
type of thing.

00:14:15.494 --> 00:14:17.250
It also connects
better to Analytics

00:14:17.250 --> 00:14:18.602
than just Realtime Database was.

00:14:18.602 --> 00:14:20.560
You'd have to be sending
your own events to say

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:22.280
people are doing this and that.

00:14:22.280 --> 00:14:24.181
But Remote Config
just ties right in.

00:14:24.181 --> 00:14:24.930
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:14:24.930 --> 00:14:27.230
DOUG STEVENSON: I think
the other thing to note

00:14:27.230 --> 00:14:31.340
is that Realtime Database is
good for storing model data,

00:14:31.340 --> 00:14:33.420
whereas Remote Config
is better for just

00:14:33.420 --> 00:14:35.400
sort of like, regular
key value pairs.

00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:37.170
And you also get
like a nice dashboard

00:14:37.170 --> 00:14:39.461
to make the changes in that,
whereas with Database, you

00:14:39.461 --> 00:14:41.390
have to go into
the database viewer

00:14:41.390 --> 00:14:43.290
and drill down into
the item that you want

00:14:43.290 --> 00:14:46.460
to change and then change it,
and it doesn't feel as right,

00:14:46.460 --> 00:14:46.960
you know.

00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:48.680
ABE HASKINS: And I think that's
an interesting discussion

00:14:48.680 --> 00:14:50.890
with the new Firebase
feature that we announced

00:14:50.890 --> 00:14:52.930
at I/O. Some of them
are targeted for more

00:14:52.930 --> 00:14:55.870
technical people, like the
Database or Authentication,

00:14:55.870 --> 00:14:58.950
some are targeted at more
marketers or different business

00:14:58.950 --> 00:14:59.820
level folks.

00:14:59.820 --> 00:15:03.200
And I think Remote Config
is walking that line, where

00:15:03.200 --> 00:15:05.250
if I give you access
to the database,

00:15:05.250 --> 00:15:08.269
and you're my boss's
boss, and you know nothing

00:15:08.269 --> 00:15:09.810
about our database
structure, there's

00:15:09.810 --> 00:15:11.300
a really good chance you're
going to break something.

00:15:11.300 --> 00:15:13.250
But if I give you
access to Remote Config

00:15:13.250 --> 00:15:15.530
and there's only a
few different ways

00:15:15.530 --> 00:15:17.420
you can set things,
and configure audiences

00:15:17.420 --> 00:15:19.770
and whatnot, that's
a marketing problem.

00:15:19.770 --> 00:15:21.990
You guys can play with
that and get that right,

00:15:21.990 --> 00:15:24.410
and I'll deal with all the
technical structured things

00:15:24.410 --> 00:15:27.730
that if you break you're going
to actually take stuff down,

00:15:27.730 --> 00:15:28.522
or what have you.

00:15:28.522 --> 00:15:29.480
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I see.

00:15:29.480 --> 00:15:31.890
So since you mentioned
it, could you

00:15:31.890 --> 00:15:33.390
talk a little bit
of Authentication?

00:15:33.390 --> 00:15:36.500
Because that's actually probably
my second favorite feature

00:15:36.500 --> 00:15:38.420
of Firebase.

00:15:38.420 --> 00:15:41.450
The first one is clearly the
Database, but Authentication,

00:15:41.450 --> 00:15:43.980
the fact that I don't have to
manage OAuth 2.0 on my site,

00:15:43.980 --> 00:15:44.770
that is amazing.

00:15:44.770 --> 00:15:47.260
Could you tell us a little
bit more about that?

00:15:47.260 --> 00:15:50.120
ABE HASKINS: Yeah, so have
you have you guys ever used

00:15:50.120 --> 00:15:52.190
like OAuth to set up,
like, Twitter login,

00:15:52.190 --> 00:15:53.042
or Facebook login?

00:15:53.042 --> 00:15:54.750
MARK MANDEL: I've
tried very hard not to.

00:15:54.750 --> 00:15:55.240
ABE HASKINS: Yeah, exactly.

00:15:55.240 --> 00:15:57.239
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I've
done it a couple of times.

00:15:57.239 --> 00:15:59.790
ABE HASKINS: I think most
people have had about nine times

00:15:59.790 --> 00:16:02.310
of trying not to, and then
one time of, like, succeeding,

00:16:02.310 --> 00:16:04.730
but really hating
themselves for it.

00:16:04.730 --> 00:16:06.800
The Firebase
Authentication is just

00:16:06.800 --> 00:16:09.730
an attempt to make that the
experience it should be.

00:16:09.730 --> 00:16:12.256
In that you, as a
developer, if you just

00:16:12.256 --> 00:16:13.630
want to sign in
with Twitter, you

00:16:13.630 --> 00:16:15.546
shouldn't have to be
running your own servers.

00:16:15.546 --> 00:16:18.220
You shouldn't have to deal
with these complicated OAuth

00:16:18.220 --> 00:16:21.090
handshakes that have, like,
three or four steps in them.

00:16:21.090 --> 00:16:23.120
You should be able to
say, sign in with Twitter,

00:16:23.120 --> 00:16:26.280
and then have the application
pop out to the Twitter app,

00:16:26.280 --> 00:16:29.690
do whatever it has to do
to get that authentication

00:16:29.690 --> 00:16:32.330
flow complete and get
that back to your app.

00:16:32.330 --> 00:16:34.260
And that's what Firebase
Authentication does.

00:16:34.260 --> 00:16:36.020
In most cases, it's
a couple of lines

00:16:36.020 --> 00:16:37.840
to do with this authentication.

00:16:37.840 --> 00:16:41.500
In the web version, it's one
line to pop up an OAuth window.

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:43.350
We dramatically
simplify the flow

00:16:43.350 --> 00:16:46.230
of integrating with third
party providers, specifically

00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:48.530
GitHub, Facebook, Google, and--

00:16:48.530 --> 00:16:49.530
DOUG STEVENSON: Twitter.

00:16:49.530 --> 00:16:51.590
ABE HASKINS: Twitter, yes.

00:16:51.590 --> 00:16:52.140
Yeah.

00:16:52.140 --> 00:16:54.874
And with that authentication,
we also offer,

00:16:54.874 --> 00:16:56.290
if you don't like
those providers,

00:16:56.290 --> 00:16:58.670
the ability to generate
your own tokens

00:16:58.670 --> 00:17:01.410
and tie into your
existing authentication

00:17:01.410 --> 00:17:02.989
back ends if you have them.

00:17:02.989 --> 00:17:05.030
DOUG STEVENSON: And I do
love the new feature now

00:17:05.030 --> 00:17:07.186
that you can do Federated
Auth, so that if people

00:17:07.186 --> 00:17:09.560
want to log in with separate
accounts for different OAuth

00:17:09.560 --> 00:17:12.109
providers, you now provide the
hook so that you can just join

00:17:12.109 --> 00:17:13.410
those together really nicely.

00:17:13.410 --> 00:17:15.500
ABE HASKINS: That's
been heavily requested.

00:17:15.500 --> 00:17:17.375
That's definitely on
the sheet of things that

00:17:17.375 --> 00:17:19.252
have been said so many times.

00:17:19.252 --> 00:17:20.960
We're working on it,
we're working on it.

00:17:20.960 --> 00:17:23.060
And I don't think people
believed us anymore.

00:17:23.060 --> 00:17:24.579
And then we came
out with it, so.

00:17:24.579 --> 00:17:25.328
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:17:25.328 --> 00:17:26.950
So there are also
several new features

00:17:26.950 --> 00:17:29.640
you have that you've
kind of absorbed

00:17:29.640 --> 00:17:31.937
into Firebase from externally.

00:17:31.937 --> 00:17:34.020
Cloud Messaging comes to
mind as well as Test Lab.

00:17:34.020 --> 00:17:35.410
Do you want to talk
about those two as well,

00:17:35.410 --> 00:17:37.790
and how they got pulled
in, and like, are there

00:17:37.790 --> 00:17:40.230
differences now, are they
the same, what changed there?

00:17:40.230 --> 00:17:41.040
ABE HASKINS: I
think I can probably

00:17:41.040 --> 00:17:42.789
speak about Cloud
Messaging, and then Doug

00:17:42.789 --> 00:17:45.180
knows much more about
Test Lab than I do.

00:17:45.180 --> 00:17:48.010
Cloud Messaging, Firebase Cloud
Messaging, or Google Cloud

00:17:48.010 --> 00:17:49.770
Messaging, serve
the same purpose.

00:17:49.770 --> 00:17:52.330
Like you said, we kind
of absorbed it in,

00:17:52.330 --> 00:17:54.657
but we made it more
Firebasey at the same time.

00:17:54.657 --> 00:17:56.490
MARK MANDEL: I want to
know what that means.

00:17:56.490 --> 00:17:57.160
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Firebasey.

00:17:57.160 --> 00:17:59.480
ABE HASKINS: Yeah, what that
means from our perspective

00:17:59.480 --> 00:18:02.220
is we made it easier to use,
we made it integrate better

00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:05.080
with the rest of
our suite of tools.

00:18:05.080 --> 00:18:08.532
It's not just a GCM
with a letter changed.

00:18:08.532 --> 00:18:10.740
We went through and we
rethought a bunch of the APIs,

00:18:10.740 --> 00:18:11.823
and we thought, all right.

00:18:11.823 --> 00:18:14.430
We have this existing Firebase
SDK you're already importing.

00:18:14.430 --> 00:18:15.660
How much of this
can we do for you?

00:18:15.660 --> 00:18:16.620
How can we simplify this?

00:18:16.620 --> 00:18:18.870
How can we make a tie into
your Analytics and Firebase

00:18:18.870 --> 00:18:20.660
Notifications?

00:18:20.660 --> 00:18:22.230
So we improved it in that way.

00:18:22.230 --> 00:18:25.100
It's much easier to get up and
running with than GCM ever was.

00:18:25.100 --> 00:18:27.100
MARK MANDEL: So take it
a back a step for a sec.

00:18:27.100 --> 00:18:28.827
What exactly was
Google Cloud Messaging?

00:18:28.827 --> 00:18:31.160
Like, what does it do for
people, in case people haven't

00:18:31.160 --> 00:18:31.970
seen that before?

00:18:31.970 --> 00:18:33.430
ABE HASKINS: So
Cloud Messaging is

00:18:33.430 --> 00:18:37.150
a way of sending push
notifications to devices.

00:18:37.150 --> 00:18:40.252
So some of those pop
up and make noises--

00:18:40.252 --> 00:18:41.710
MARK MANDEL: That
was great timing.

00:18:43.744 --> 00:18:45.660
ABE HASKINS: Yeah, so
some of those you pop up

00:18:45.660 --> 00:18:47.470
and you see, it
might say, hey, you

00:18:47.470 --> 00:18:49.090
should come beat
level 3 of this game.

00:18:49.090 --> 00:18:52.540
Some of them happen behind
the scenes and trigger things

00:18:52.540 --> 00:18:54.290
in your app, but it's
basically just a way

00:18:54.290 --> 00:18:56.690
of pushing notifications
to devices.

00:18:56.690 --> 00:18:57.430
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:18:57.430 --> 00:18:59.000
So as you were
saying, like now it's

00:18:59.000 --> 00:19:00.860
a lot easier to use,
and a lot better, and--

00:19:00.860 --> 00:19:01.490
ABE HASKINS: Yeah, exactly.

00:19:01.490 --> 00:19:01.890
MARK MANDEL: --cleaner.

00:19:01.890 --> 00:19:04.431
ABE HASKINS: Yeah, it's never
been a horribly difficult thing

00:19:04.431 --> 00:19:07.424
to use, but wherever we could
when we rolled products in,

00:19:07.424 --> 00:19:09.340
we improved the APIs,
improved the experience,

00:19:09.340 --> 00:19:11.669
and integrated them as
well as we possibly could.

00:19:11.669 --> 00:19:13.085
DOUG STEVENSON:
And that's related

00:19:13.085 --> 00:19:16.140
to Notifications too, which
is now a different product.

00:19:16.140 --> 00:19:17.980
ABE HASKINS: So,
yeah, this probably

00:19:17.980 --> 00:19:21.790
requires saying very
explicitly, there's

00:19:21.790 --> 00:19:25.300
been confusion around Firebase
Cloud Messaging versus Firebase

00:19:25.300 --> 00:19:26.320
Notifications.

00:19:26.320 --> 00:19:27.640
And this is fair.

00:19:27.640 --> 00:19:30.640
It's a little confusing, which
is which, and how they relate.

00:19:30.640 --> 00:19:35.660
But you can think of Firebase
Cloud Messaging as the API,

00:19:35.660 --> 00:19:38.520
the SDK, that's on your
phone, that you send down

00:19:38.520 --> 00:19:41.240
to your clients that lets
you receive messages.

00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:43.200
You can also programmatically
send messages

00:19:43.200 --> 00:19:46.500
through FCM from your existing
server if you wanted to.

00:19:46.500 --> 00:19:48.600
Now FCM goes back to that split.

00:19:48.600 --> 00:19:49.810
I mean, sorry, not FCM.

00:19:49.810 --> 00:19:51.480
The Notification
concept goes back

00:19:51.480 --> 00:19:54.570
to that split I was talking
about between Firebase products

00:19:54.570 --> 00:19:57.080
for technical folks, and
Firebase products for more

00:19:57.080 --> 00:19:59.080
marketing business folks.

00:19:59.080 --> 00:20:00.780
So if you have
audiences set up, if you

00:20:00.780 --> 00:20:04.090
have your analytics configured,
or different groups you want

00:20:04.090 --> 00:20:06.135
to filter and send
notifications to,

00:20:06.135 --> 00:20:08.260
historically, you'd have
to use a third party tool.

00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:10.930
You'd have to go and rely
on your analytics platform

00:20:10.930 --> 00:20:14.280
to give you the ability to send
notifications to these people.

00:20:14.280 --> 00:20:17.160
That's what cloud-- what
Firebase Notifications does

00:20:17.160 --> 00:20:17.660
for you.

00:20:17.660 --> 00:20:21.110
It's a nice interface, it's
a UI for non-technical folks

00:20:21.110 --> 00:20:23.690
to go in and say, I want
to target this group,

00:20:23.690 --> 00:20:25.540
I want to send messages
to these people,

00:20:25.540 --> 00:20:27.831
people who speak these
languages get this notification,

00:20:27.831 --> 00:20:28.470
or whatever.

00:20:28.470 --> 00:20:29.450
MARK MANDEL: So
Messaging is more

00:20:29.450 --> 00:20:31.080
for the technical
programmatic people,

00:20:31.080 --> 00:20:32.640
whereas the Notification
stuff's probably

00:20:32.640 --> 00:20:33.806
more for the less technical.

00:20:33.806 --> 00:20:35.227
ABE HASKINS: Yeah, exactly.

00:20:35.227 --> 00:20:37.310
DOUG STEVENSON: An important
distinguishing factor

00:20:37.310 --> 00:20:40.650
between them is
Firebase Cloud Messaging

00:20:40.650 --> 00:20:42.492
is more for like
event driven messages,

00:20:42.492 --> 00:20:44.700
so you have something that--
like a notification that

00:20:44.700 --> 00:20:46.440
happens in response to
something else that happened

00:20:46.440 --> 00:20:47.981
in your system,
whereas Notifications

00:20:47.981 --> 00:20:51.567
is more like not user
to user, but, you know,

00:20:51.567 --> 00:20:52.400
app manager to user.

00:20:52.400 --> 00:20:54.310
Like, I want to type a
message in the console

00:20:54.310 --> 00:20:56.330
and send it to my user
so they can see it.

00:20:56.330 --> 00:20:57.530
So that's that case.

00:20:57.530 --> 00:20:59.630
ABE HASKINS: Yeah, and
they both come back to FCM.

00:20:59.630 --> 00:21:03.350
Like, you're never going
to import the Firebase

00:21:03.350 --> 00:21:05.622
Notifications into your SDK.

00:21:05.622 --> 00:21:07.580
Once you put Firebase
Cloud Messaging in there,

00:21:07.580 --> 00:21:11.200
you have the ability to use
Notifications to send messages

00:21:11.200 --> 00:21:12.206
down to your devices.

00:21:12.206 --> 00:21:13.164
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:21:13.164 --> 00:21:14.140
And Test Lab.

00:21:14.140 --> 00:21:16.510
DOUG STEVENSON: Yeah,
so it's formally

00:21:16.510 --> 00:21:18.360
called Firebase
Test Lab for Android

00:21:18.360 --> 00:21:20.570
because it only supports
Android devices right now.

00:21:20.570 --> 00:21:21.470
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:21:21.470 --> 00:21:24.169
DOUG STEVENSON: But this is
basically the same Cloud Test

00:21:24.169 --> 00:21:26.210
Lab product that's been
moved over into Firebase,

00:21:26.210 --> 00:21:29.230
so now you can do the same
things you could before.

00:21:29.230 --> 00:21:33.100
So there's a robo test, which
is like a fully automated test

00:21:33.100 --> 00:21:35.170
that we have this software
agent that basically

00:21:35.170 --> 00:21:36.370
works your app for you.

00:21:36.370 --> 00:21:38.300
It sort of discovers
UI widgets and buttons,

00:21:38.300 --> 00:21:40.622
and pokes and prods, and
sort of systematically

00:21:40.622 --> 00:21:42.580
crawls your app to find
all the features, which

00:21:42.580 --> 00:21:44.190
is pretty cool because you
don't write a line of code

00:21:44.190 --> 00:21:44.690
to do that.

00:21:44.690 --> 00:21:47.345
You get that-- you get
something kind of for nothing.

00:21:47.345 --> 00:21:49.720
Which is great, because if
you don't want to write tests,

00:21:49.720 --> 00:21:51.428
and-- I know I don't
want to write tests.

00:21:51.428 --> 00:21:53.644
Tests are not exactly
like fun to write.

00:21:53.644 --> 00:21:55.060
MARK MANDEL: I
like writing tests.

00:21:55.060 --> 00:21:56.840
I really enjoy writing tests.

00:21:56.840 --> 00:21:59.417
FRANCESC CAMPOY: You also
like writing Haskell.

00:21:59.417 --> 00:22:01.000
DOUG STEVENSON: So
for people like you

00:22:01.000 --> 00:22:03.604
who enjoy writing tests,
if you enjoy writing tests,

00:22:03.604 --> 00:22:04.270
you can do that.

00:22:04.270 --> 00:22:06.270
You can write Android
instrumentation tests as well,

00:22:06.270 --> 00:22:07.686
and execute that
against your app.

00:22:07.686 --> 00:22:09.880
So you can upload both
APKs, your app APK

00:22:09.880 --> 00:22:13.810
and your instrumentation test,
and we'll run those as well.

00:22:13.810 --> 00:22:16.401
Let's see, what else
can we do with Test Lab?

00:22:16.401 --> 00:22:16.900
There's--

00:22:16.900 --> 00:22:17.620
MARK MANDEL: I'm
guessing you can

00:22:17.620 --> 00:22:20.335
run across various different
devices as well in sizes--

00:22:20.335 --> 00:22:20.670
DOUG STEVENSON: Yeah.

00:22:20.670 --> 00:22:21.050
MARK MANDEL: --And
shapes and stuff?

00:22:21.050 --> 00:22:24.260
DOUG STEVENSON: Yeah, so that's
what we call the device test

00:22:24.260 --> 00:22:26.150
matrix, so what you
do is you select

00:22:26.150 --> 00:22:28.344
the devices, the actual
models of devices

00:22:28.344 --> 00:22:29.760
that you're
interested in testing.

00:22:29.760 --> 00:22:33.150
You select the API levels,
the Android API levels.

00:22:33.150 --> 00:22:35.250
Not every device
supports every API level,

00:22:35.250 --> 00:22:38.620
but we'll find an intersecting
combination that will fit.

00:22:38.620 --> 00:22:42.140
You can test in portrait,
you can test in landscape,

00:22:42.140 --> 00:22:44.480
and so based on your
selections, we'll

00:22:44.480 --> 00:22:46.520
create a series of
tests that actually

00:22:46.520 --> 00:22:47.769
satisfies your requirements.

00:22:47.769 --> 00:22:48.810
MARK MANDEL: That's cool.

00:22:48.810 --> 00:22:50.740
And do these run
on real devices,

00:22:50.740 --> 00:22:52.995
or is it all emulated,
or, how does that work?

00:22:52.995 --> 00:22:55.120
DOUG STEVENSON: Yeah, so
these are actually devices

00:22:55.120 --> 00:22:56.770
sitting in a rack in the Cloud.

00:22:56.770 --> 00:22:59.912
You could think of it like
Cloud hosted or managed devices.

00:22:59.912 --> 00:23:01.620
So you don't ever have
to touch a device.

00:23:01.620 --> 00:23:05.060
You don't have to have tangled
cables on your computer.

00:23:05.060 --> 00:23:08.286
Back when I did testing as
a startup a long time ago,

00:23:08.286 --> 00:23:10.036
it was like you had
this stack of devices,

00:23:10.036 --> 00:23:11.990
you were constantly like, let's
do this one now, let's try

00:23:11.990 --> 00:23:12.970
this one, let's try this one.

00:23:12.970 --> 00:23:14.428
And I only have
one USB port, and I

00:23:14.428 --> 00:23:15.710
can't manage the whole thing.

00:23:15.710 --> 00:23:16.840
So now, you don't
have to do that.

00:23:16.840 --> 00:23:18.256
You can run all--
you're basically

00:23:18.256 --> 00:23:20.048
delegating all of your
tests into the Cloud

00:23:20.048 --> 00:23:22.381
and then we'll send you test
results when they're ready.

00:23:22.381 --> 00:23:24.940
And they're running in
parallel, so for as many devices

00:23:24.940 --> 00:23:27.360
as you've selected, we
will try to use them

00:23:27.360 --> 00:23:29.142
as fast as we possibly can.

00:23:29.142 --> 00:23:30.850
MARK MANDEL: And did
this platform change

00:23:30.850 --> 00:23:33.830
as it got absorbed by
Firebase, or is it still fairly

00:23:33.830 --> 00:23:35.190
much the same, or--

00:23:35.190 --> 00:23:37.930
DOUG STEVENSON: The platform
itself is mostly the same.

00:23:37.930 --> 00:23:43.060
The big change was that we had
a UI Cloud Developer Platform

00:23:43.060 --> 00:23:43.972
Console.

00:23:43.972 --> 00:23:45.680
Now that's been moved
over into Firebase.

00:23:45.680 --> 00:23:49.780
So now, you see Test Lab along
with all your other products.

00:23:49.780 --> 00:23:52.940
I think the UI is better,
it's little easier to use,

00:23:52.940 --> 00:23:55.320
but for the most part, the
infrastructure is the same.

00:23:55.320 --> 00:23:57.630
Now there is one new thing
that goes along with this.

00:23:57.630 --> 00:24:00.000
It's not part of
Test Lab proper,

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:01.720
it's actually part
of Android Studio,

00:24:01.720 --> 00:24:04.440
and this is coming with
Android Studio 2.2.

00:24:04.440 --> 00:24:06.080
It is this product
called Cassette.

00:24:06.080 --> 00:24:08.690
And what it lets you do is
launch your app on a device,

00:24:08.690 --> 00:24:12.140
then you use it, you actually
type in, push the buttons,

00:24:12.140 --> 00:24:14.630
enter the data for the
test the way you want to,

00:24:14.630 --> 00:24:17.004
and then what it will do is
record everything you've done

00:24:17.004 --> 00:24:18.550
and create Espresso
tests for you.

00:24:18.550 --> 00:24:21.597
So you don't actually have to
write out all of the on view,

00:24:21.597 --> 00:24:23.930
click this button, then that
thing, and check that text,

00:24:23.930 --> 00:24:26.360
it's all done in a UI,
which is super handy.

00:24:26.360 --> 00:24:28.670
You can grind through
a lot of test cases

00:24:28.670 --> 00:24:29.750
very quickly that way.

00:24:29.750 --> 00:24:30.930
MARK MANDEL: You say
it was called Cassette?

00:24:30.930 --> 00:24:31.490
DOUG STEVENSON: Cassette, yes.

00:24:31.490 --> 00:24:32.330
MARK MANDEL: As in,
like, what I used

00:24:32.330 --> 00:24:34.245
to listen to music on
when I was a teenager?

00:24:34.245 --> 00:24:36.369
DOUG STEVENSON: Yes, oh,
I used to save my programs

00:24:36.369 --> 00:24:37.552
on when I was a kid.

00:24:37.552 --> 00:24:38.343
Yes, I am that old.

00:24:38.343 --> 00:24:41.061
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Wow, people are old.

00:24:41.061 --> 00:24:41.970
Cool.

00:24:41.970 --> 00:24:43.760
We're pretty much
running out of time.

00:24:43.760 --> 00:24:46.190
But there was something that
I wanted to discuss, too,

00:24:46.190 --> 00:24:49.470
was Storage, and how is it
different to Google Cloud

00:24:49.470 --> 00:24:52.800
Storage, if any
difference exists?

00:24:52.800 --> 00:24:55.080
ABE HASKINS: So it is
different in the way

00:24:55.080 --> 00:24:56.170
you interact with it.

00:24:56.170 --> 00:24:58.310
Firebase Storage is backed
by Google Cloud Storage.

00:24:58.310 --> 00:25:00.060
When you sign up
for Firebase, you

00:25:00.060 --> 00:25:03.930
get a Firebase-- I mean, a
Google Cloud bucket that you

00:25:03.930 --> 00:25:05.190
can interact with.

00:25:05.190 --> 00:25:07.200
You can even open it
in the Cloud console.

00:25:07.200 --> 00:25:09.680
And if you have
existing apps that

00:25:09.680 --> 00:25:13.820
use existing APIs to interact
with the Google Cloud bucket,

00:25:13.820 --> 00:25:17.270
you can interact with
it from your Firebase.

00:25:17.270 --> 00:25:19.300
I mean, your Firebase
bucket can be

00:25:19.300 --> 00:25:21.300
interacted within the same way.

00:25:21.300 --> 00:25:24.740
The benefit of using Firebase
Storage over Cloud Storage

00:25:24.740 --> 00:25:26.240
is that Firebase
Storage is designed

00:25:26.240 --> 00:25:28.220
to be used from clients.

00:25:28.220 --> 00:25:31.610
Google Cloud Storage never
really had a good solution

00:25:31.610 --> 00:25:35.180
to I have a user, I
want to upload an image,

00:25:35.180 --> 00:25:38.370
I want to upload directly to
that bucket from my web page

00:25:38.370 --> 00:25:39.200
or from my app.

00:25:39.200 --> 00:25:41.230
You'd have to bounce it
through your own server.

00:25:41.230 --> 00:25:45.180
You'd have to do checks on the
file type and the file size

00:25:45.180 --> 00:25:46.190
and things like that.

00:25:46.190 --> 00:25:49.280
And eventually, you could get
in the Cloud Storage bucket,

00:25:49.280 --> 00:25:51.730
but it was kind of wonky.

00:25:51.730 --> 00:25:55.060
Firebase Storage introduces
two really cool concepts.

00:25:55.060 --> 00:25:57.990
One is the client side SDK for
doing these types of uploads

00:25:57.990 --> 00:26:00.830
so you can just say, hey,
upload this securely and safely

00:26:00.830 --> 00:26:04.600
directly to my Firebase Storage
and the underlying Google Cloud

00:26:04.600 --> 00:26:05.630
Storage bucket.

00:26:05.630 --> 00:26:08.430
And the other is
the security rules

00:26:08.430 --> 00:26:11.810
that allow you to say, OK,
this person can only upload

00:26:11.810 --> 00:26:13.500
to this path inside my bucket.

00:26:13.500 --> 00:26:15.630
They can only overwrite
their own profile image,

00:26:15.630 --> 00:26:18.690
they can only be-- I don't
know if this is out currently,

00:26:18.690 --> 00:26:20.890
or if we're working on
this now, but the ability

00:26:20.890 --> 00:26:23.971
to restrict file sizes, image
sizes, things like that.

00:26:23.971 --> 00:26:25.720
So you can say a profile
image can only be

00:26:25.720 --> 00:26:27.910
this big or things like that.

00:26:27.910 --> 00:26:29.700
So there's a lot of
benefits in that,

00:26:29.700 --> 00:26:31.510
like I said before, these
are things you could do.

00:26:31.510 --> 00:26:32.676
They're like Authentication.

00:26:32.676 --> 00:26:34.391
You could do the
OAuth flow, or you

00:26:34.391 --> 00:26:36.390
could do this and save
yourself a bunch of time.

00:26:36.390 --> 00:26:37.150
Same with this.

00:26:37.150 --> 00:26:39.210
Using just Google
Cloud Storage, you

00:26:39.210 --> 00:26:41.790
could have sent everything
through your own server, gotten

00:26:41.790 --> 00:26:44.312
to the same point, but we
just save you a ton of time.

00:26:44.312 --> 00:26:46.270
DOUG STEVENSON: Now those
Authentication rules.

00:26:46.270 --> 00:26:49.350
Those are just like very similar
to, if not exactly the same,

00:26:49.350 --> 00:26:51.119
as Firebase Realtime Database.

00:26:51.119 --> 00:26:53.160
ABE HASKINS: Yeah, so
they're definitely similar.

00:26:53.160 --> 00:26:55.860
They're not the same syntax.

00:26:55.860 --> 00:26:59.915
They are specifically
used for storage,

00:26:59.915 --> 00:27:02.290
like, there's very different
requirements for the storage

00:27:02.290 --> 00:27:04.230
rules versus the
Firebase security rules.

00:27:04.230 --> 00:27:06.810
But if you've ever written
database security rules,

00:27:06.810 --> 00:27:09.870
you'll feel very at home
using this very descriptive,

00:27:09.870 --> 00:27:12.170
nicely typed thing, to
say, oh, this location,

00:27:12.170 --> 00:27:15.040
I'm logged in as this user,
I can write here, I can't.

00:27:15.040 --> 00:27:17.627
It's very, very sane, and
you have all your rules

00:27:17.627 --> 00:27:19.210
in one place, so
it's easy to look at.

00:27:19.210 --> 00:27:20.972
In the same way, they're
the same benefits

00:27:20.972 --> 00:27:22.680
you get from the
database security rules.

00:27:22.680 --> 00:27:24.305
MARK MANDEL: Yeah I
know when I used it

00:27:24.305 --> 00:27:25.860
for my presentation
I gave at I/O,

00:27:25.860 --> 00:27:27.629
and it feels like
interacting with Storage

00:27:27.629 --> 00:27:29.920
is almost very similar to
interacting with the Realtime

00:27:29.920 --> 00:27:30.360
Database.

00:27:30.360 --> 00:27:31.980
You end up with the
same instructions,

00:27:31.980 --> 00:27:34.830
and it's super nice to use,
and probably one of the nicest

00:27:34.830 --> 00:27:37.330
ways I've ever used an
interaction with the Google

00:27:37.330 --> 00:27:38.480
Cloud Storage.

00:27:38.480 --> 00:27:40.080
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So in the same way

00:27:40.080 --> 00:27:42.670
that if something in
the database changes,

00:27:42.670 --> 00:27:45.910
you can watch that change
and receive a notification,

00:27:45.910 --> 00:27:47.990
can you do the same
thing for storage?

00:27:47.990 --> 00:27:49.780
ABE HASKINS: Not really.

00:27:49.780 --> 00:27:53.050
What you can do is you can
say, I'm going to upload this,

00:27:53.050 --> 00:27:55.530
and then you'll have a location
in the Realtime Database,

00:27:55.530 --> 00:27:58.660
and when your upload is
complete, you flip a switch,

00:27:58.660 --> 00:28:00.910
say, oh there's a new version
and everyone can update.

00:28:00.910 --> 00:28:04.617
So we don't have the ability to
do that type of updating today.

00:28:04.617 --> 00:28:05.450
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:28:05.450 --> 00:28:06.825
Because that's
something, yeah, I

00:28:06.825 --> 00:28:08.666
could have them-- I was
thinking about that.

00:28:08.666 --> 00:28:11.290
DOUG STEVENSON: There's one cool
thing you can do with Storage.

00:28:11.290 --> 00:28:13.760
So on the client
SDK side, you can

00:28:13.760 --> 00:28:16.099
do-- you can kick off
an upload, and if it's

00:28:16.099 --> 00:28:18.390
going to take a long time,
or if you lose connectivity,

00:28:18.390 --> 00:28:19.930
it will automatically
resume it because it's

00:28:19.930 --> 00:28:21.400
meant for large
files, it's meant

00:28:21.400 --> 00:28:22.914
for user generated content.

00:28:22.914 --> 00:28:24.580
So if users are taking
a bunch of photos

00:28:24.580 --> 00:28:26.560
and you want to upload
them all, we don't have to,

00:28:26.560 --> 00:28:28.310
like, sit there and
make them wait for it.

00:28:28.310 --> 00:28:30.890
You can go into the background,
complete in the background,

00:28:30.890 --> 00:28:33.750
and be sure that if they go to
a tunnel, lose connectivity,

00:28:33.750 --> 00:28:35.529
that's fine, we'll restart.

00:28:35.529 --> 00:28:37.320
But it's also fully
secure, so everything's

00:28:37.320 --> 00:28:38.278
over secure connection.

00:28:38.278 --> 00:28:40.340
ABE HASKINS: Yeah,
the Firebase Storage

00:28:40.340 --> 00:28:42.540
is definitely the
product that just

00:28:42.540 --> 00:28:45.480
feels like classic
Firebase I think the most.

00:28:45.480 --> 00:28:47.650
It's built with
things like that,

00:28:47.650 --> 00:28:50.610
like that resilience that
the Realtime Database had

00:28:50.610 --> 00:28:51.670
was always a focus.

00:28:51.670 --> 00:28:53.920
The ability-- you can go
through a tunnel and your app

00:28:53.920 --> 00:28:54.530
keeps working.

00:28:54.530 --> 00:28:56.981
Storage we wanted to be the
same way, and it very much is.

00:28:56.981 --> 00:28:59.480
It goes back to the thing, like,
you could do that yourself,

00:28:59.480 --> 00:29:00.700
but it would be a pain.

00:29:00.700 --> 00:29:02.170
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, absolutely.

