00:31:08.410 --> 00:31:09.940
So you wanted to
have a little chat

00:31:09.940 --> 00:31:13.120
about how do we debug stuff.

00:31:13.120 --> 00:31:15.230
That's a very broad statement.

00:31:15.230 --> 00:31:16.270
What do you mean there?

00:31:16.270 --> 00:31:18.186
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It is
a very broad statement,

00:31:18.186 --> 00:31:20.450
but basically the question
was something around, OK,

00:31:20.450 --> 00:31:23.630
so something's wrong
with my application

00:31:23.630 --> 00:31:26.470
and I'm not very
sure what it is.

00:31:26.470 --> 00:31:27.790
What are my options?

00:31:27.790 --> 00:31:28.660
What do I do?

00:31:28.660 --> 00:31:29.682
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:31:29.682 --> 00:31:31.432
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
basically, the answer

00:31:31.432 --> 00:31:34.421
could be something like,
well, write more unit tests.

00:31:34.421 --> 00:31:34.920
Right?

00:31:34.920 --> 00:31:38.940
But the problem is that
what if what is going wrong

00:31:38.940 --> 00:31:40.977
it's only going
wrong in production?

00:31:40.977 --> 00:31:42.060
MARK MANDEL: That happens.

00:31:42.060 --> 00:31:42.720
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That happens.

00:31:42.720 --> 00:31:43.080
MARK MANDEL: That happens.

00:31:43.080 --> 00:31:44.829
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Sadly
happens very often,

00:31:44.829 --> 00:31:46.120
that it works on my machine.

00:31:46.120 --> 00:31:48.520
It's not a thing, really.

00:31:48.520 --> 00:31:52.660
So what I wanted to--
basically, the answer

00:31:52.660 --> 00:31:54.314
I wanted to give
to this was there's

00:31:54.314 --> 00:31:55.730
a lot of different
things that you

00:31:55.730 --> 00:31:58.500
can do to basically
investigate what

00:31:58.500 --> 00:32:00.600
is a problem in
application and production.

00:32:00.600 --> 00:32:03.850
And most of them are
actually under these features

00:32:03.850 --> 00:32:05.080
that we call Stackdriver.

00:32:05.080 --> 00:32:05.830
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:32:05.830 --> 00:32:07.450
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
Stackdriver has a bunch

00:32:07.450 --> 00:32:08.533
of things that you can do.

00:32:08.533 --> 00:32:10.250
One of them, the
first one is-- OK,

00:32:10.250 --> 00:32:12.692
so if something is
going wrong, there's

00:32:12.692 --> 00:32:13.900
two different kinds of wrong.

00:32:13.900 --> 00:32:15.910
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, I was going
to say, like, what does wrong

00:32:15.910 --> 00:32:16.727
mean, right?

00:32:16.727 --> 00:32:18.310
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So,
one of the wrongs

00:32:18.310 --> 00:32:20.402
is you were actually
getting errors.

00:32:20.402 --> 00:32:21.110
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:32:21.110 --> 00:32:22.830
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
And in that case,

00:32:22.830 --> 00:32:25.660
you're going to see
that Stackdriver

00:32:25.660 --> 00:32:27.902
has a way of monitoring
those errors--

00:32:27.902 --> 00:32:28.610
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:32:28.610 --> 00:32:29.980
FRANCESC CAMPOY: --and
putting them together

00:32:29.980 --> 00:32:31.160
according to some template.

00:32:31.160 --> 00:32:34.160
So you can actually
get that, hey,

00:32:34.160 --> 00:32:37.720
during the last
hour, 500 occurrences

00:32:37.720 --> 00:32:40.060
of these specific
kind of error happen.

00:32:40.060 --> 00:32:42.250
You can create alerts
on that, and get

00:32:42.250 --> 00:32:44.880
alerted when those errors
go higher than some--

00:32:44.880 --> 00:32:45.630
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:32:45.630 --> 00:32:46.210
FRANCESC CAMPOY: --threshold.

00:32:46.210 --> 00:32:47.109
So it's very useful.

00:32:47.109 --> 00:32:49.650
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, Stackdriver
Error Reporting is quite nice.

00:32:49.650 --> 00:32:52.100
You can also do stuff like,
oh, this error just happens,

00:32:52.100 --> 00:32:53.780
and it doesn't
matter if it does.

00:32:53.780 --> 00:32:54.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
it's just like shut up.

00:32:54.180 --> 00:32:54.230
Yeah.

00:32:54.230 --> 00:32:54.280
Basically.

00:32:54.280 --> 00:32:56.190
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, but
otherwise, like, yeah,

00:32:56.190 --> 00:32:57.540
this one's actually
really important

00:32:57.540 --> 00:32:59.470
and we need to get people out
of bed for this particular one.

00:32:59.470 --> 00:32:59.690
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:32:59.690 --> 00:33:01.240
MARK MANDEL: We need that
sort of error reporting.

00:33:01.240 --> 00:33:03.420
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
then when that happens,

00:33:03.420 --> 00:33:05.080
something that you
may consider is

00:33:05.080 --> 00:33:06.663
like, well, I'm just
going to go there

00:33:06.663 --> 00:33:09.140
and maybe you have
like a Java application

00:33:09.140 --> 00:33:14.470
and I'm just going to
read the exception stack.

00:33:14.470 --> 00:33:15.056
Stack?

00:33:15.056 --> 00:33:15.806
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:33:15.806 --> 00:33:16.450
Yeah, you're good.

00:33:16.450 --> 00:33:17.190
Yeah, you're good.

00:33:17.190 --> 00:33:17.470
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I don't know.

00:33:17.470 --> 00:33:19.800
I don't do exceptions, so
I'll remember the name.

00:33:19.800 --> 00:33:21.949
So yeah, you can check
the exception stack

00:33:21.949 --> 00:33:23.740
and understand [INAUDIBLE]
what's going on,

00:33:23.740 --> 00:33:25.880
but that's sometimes not enough.

00:33:25.880 --> 00:33:29.730
And in that case, there's
this really cool thing.

00:33:29.730 --> 00:33:32.770
It's basically a
debugger in production.

00:33:32.770 --> 00:33:35.982
A debugger in production
sounds like an awful idea,

00:33:35.982 --> 00:33:37.940
because putting break
points on your production

00:33:37.940 --> 00:33:40.590
is something that you should
never even consider doing it.

00:33:40.590 --> 00:33:43.770
Please, don't do that at home.

00:33:43.770 --> 00:33:47.040
But what we have rather
than break points,

00:33:47.040 --> 00:33:48.740
we have snapshot points.

00:33:48.740 --> 00:33:50.750
So what you say is
like, hey, I want

00:33:50.750 --> 00:33:53.280
to know what are the values
of all the [INAUDIBLE]

00:33:53.280 --> 00:33:55.969
stack, and the local
fireballs, and all that stuff.

00:33:55.969 --> 00:33:58.510
MARK MANDEL: At this snapshot,
this particular point in time.

00:33:58.510 --> 00:33:59.980
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And you
can even add conditions.

00:33:59.980 --> 00:34:00.730
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:34:00.730 --> 00:34:03.210
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It was
like, when x equals 10,

00:34:03.210 --> 00:34:05.380
give me all the
values of the stuff.

00:34:05.380 --> 00:34:07.080
And then with that,
basically, you'll

00:34:07.080 --> 00:34:08.880
be able to kind of
debug in production,

00:34:08.880 --> 00:34:11.080
but without the cost
of actually stopping

00:34:11.080 --> 00:34:13.280
any jobs in production,
which is a really bad idea.

00:34:13.280 --> 00:34:15.863
MARK MANDEL: I'll actually put
a little thing on here as well.

00:34:15.863 --> 00:34:17.480
It's super, super, super alpha.

00:34:17.480 --> 00:34:20.754
But there is a plug-in for
Google Cloud Platform IntelliJ,

00:34:20.754 --> 00:34:22.170
and the integrated
debugging stuff

00:34:22.170 --> 00:34:23.545
they've got for
Java applications

00:34:23.545 --> 00:34:25.780
in that is very, very slick.

00:34:25.780 --> 00:34:28.730
Worth looking at, probably
not worth trying just yet,

00:34:28.730 --> 00:34:30.290
but worth keeping an eye on.

00:34:30.290 --> 00:34:32.245
It's super interesting.

00:34:32.245 --> 00:34:33.370
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:34:33.370 --> 00:34:35.770
And yeah, on top
of that, you also

00:34:35.770 --> 00:34:38.110
get the other kind of
wrong, which is things

00:34:38.110 --> 00:34:40.370
are just working
but just very slow.

00:34:40.370 --> 00:34:43.469
And then in that case, you
have the performance insights

00:34:43.469 --> 00:34:44.969
and analytics, so
you're going to be

00:34:44.969 --> 00:34:48.560
able to see what are
all the calls generated

00:34:48.560 --> 00:34:50.449
from your request.

00:34:50.449 --> 00:34:52.110
So imagine that
you get a request

00:34:52.110 --> 00:34:57.040
and rather than sending one
datastore read operation,

00:34:57.040 --> 00:34:58.585
you send 1,000.

00:34:58.585 --> 00:35:00.210
Maybe there's something
wrong in there.

00:35:00.210 --> 00:35:03.530
Maybe you send those
1,000s one after the other.

00:35:03.530 --> 00:35:05.850
So concurrency, my friend.

00:35:05.850 --> 00:35:08.020
Those things are
something that you

00:35:08.020 --> 00:35:11.330
can see very easily with
the detail from insights

00:35:11.330 --> 00:35:13.802
and the analytics that
Stackdriver provides.

00:35:13.802 --> 00:35:15.260
MARK MANDEL: Yes,
Stackdriver Trace

00:35:15.260 --> 00:35:17.590
is also really cool in that
it can give you alerts when

00:35:17.590 --> 00:35:19.290
performance starts to degrade.

00:35:19.290 --> 00:35:21.180
It's not just a
reactionary thing

00:35:21.180 --> 00:35:22.440
from users sending you stuff.

00:35:22.440 --> 00:35:26.710
If you start to see that
performance is happening,

00:35:26.710 --> 00:35:29.610
you can set up alerts, and say
if performance on this website

00:35:29.610 --> 00:35:32.230
drops below a certain threshold,
or something like that,

00:35:32.230 --> 00:35:33.900
then you can be
like, hey, maybe you

00:35:33.900 --> 00:35:35.250
do need to get someone up
in the middle of the night,

00:35:35.250 --> 00:35:37.300
or at least send an
e-mail, or do something.

00:35:37.300 --> 00:35:38.930
Super, super useful.

00:35:38.930 --> 00:35:41.540
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And there's
actually a very good talk

00:35:41.540 --> 00:35:43.510
that if you're
interested on this topic,

00:35:43.510 --> 00:35:45.400
you should definitely
check it out.

00:35:45.400 --> 00:35:48.370
We're going to have the link
on the show notes as usual.

00:35:48.370 --> 00:35:52.150
And it's called, "Diagnostics,
spend less time diagnosing,

00:35:52.150 --> 00:35:53.527
and more time developing."

00:35:53.527 --> 00:35:55.860
MARK MANDEL: I'll make one
mention of one extra product,

00:35:55.860 --> 00:35:57.040
because I think
it's worth noting.

00:35:57.040 --> 00:35:57.530
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Sure.

00:35:57.530 --> 00:35:59.300
MARK MANDEL: It's the
Stackdriver Logging.

00:35:59.300 --> 00:35:59.640
FRANCESC CAMPOY: True.

00:35:59.640 --> 00:36:01.270
MARK MANDEL: Super basic, right?

00:36:01.270 --> 00:36:04.040
But it is a central
place to send your logs.

00:36:04.040 --> 00:36:06.470
Like, for a lot of our
platform and servers stuff, so

00:36:06.470 --> 00:36:09.551
app engine, et cetera,
that's just built in.

00:36:09.551 --> 00:36:12.050
Like, if I want to go see all
my logs, or my community apps,

00:36:12.050 --> 00:36:14.180
or anything like that, I
can just go right there.

00:36:14.180 --> 00:36:16.972
But I can also send out custom
logs, there's API calls,

00:36:16.972 --> 00:36:18.430
I can do what
[INTERPOSING VOICES],

00:36:18.430 --> 00:36:20.263
I can do it a whole
bunch of different ways.

00:36:20.263 --> 00:36:22.055
And so, you know, log
your applications.

00:36:22.055 --> 00:36:23.513
So if you really
want to see what's

00:36:23.513 --> 00:36:25.700
going on inside your
app, just good old

00:36:25.700 --> 00:36:28.099
classic text-based
logs, sometimes

00:36:28.099 --> 00:36:29.140
